/* ================= v22 add-on: traffic — lane-level vehicles, signals and buses on the real roads (lm20/traffic) =================
   Replaces the app's generic cars (buildCars / stepCars: one InstancedMesh of boxes gliding along A* paths) in the real maps
   (Singapore and the San Francisco Bay Area, SGR.on) with a vehicle system that drives the real OSM road lines:
   - network: built lazily around the view from SGR.lines and the roads module's junction graph (SGRoads._dbg.prep: junction
     nodes on every line, junction complexes; SGRoads._dbg.cxGet: arm trims, stop-line positions, which complexes have
     signals). A carriageway piece between two junctions is a link; two-way pieces give a link per direction, one-way lines one.
     Lanes per class from the roads module's LANES table, lateral positions exactly where its lane markings are (left-hand
     traffic in Singapore, right-hand in the Bay, from SGR_BAY), centrelines smoothed with the same corner arcs, heights from the
     same cross-section (ground + the line's E profile + surface offset), so vehicles sit on flyovers, ramps and bridges. Line ends
     at the same vertex and level that the module's node test missed are joined; one-way decks that continue with a level step
     in the data (<= 15 m) are joined too and the drawn height eases over the step. Real dead ends and tunnel portals (tunnels
     are not mapped) are sinks / sources: vehicles drive into them and vanish, or come out of them.
   - junctions: movements (straight / kerb-side turn / turn across traffic / U-turn only at dead ends) with lane permissions
     (kerb turns from the kerb lane, turns across from the far lane, diverges and lane drops spread over the lanes), curved
     connectors (quadratic Bezier between the lane positions) through the junction box, conflicts from the arms' angular order
     (chords on the junction circle), priorities (the major road; minor roads give way; US minor roads stop), zipper merges at
     slip roads, a box reservation so crossing paths never overlap, and "don't block the box" (room on the exit lane).
   - signals: every complex the roads module marks as signalised (the ones with stop lines and zebra crossings) runs a fixed
     cycle (main road, protected turn across traffic, side road, its turn; amber, all-red), offset per junction; the manoeuvre
     through a multi-node complex (dual carriageways) is judged from entry to exit; vehicles stop at the painted stop line;
     signal poles with lit heads at the kerb of each approach (a mast arm and a second head over approaches of 2+ lanes).
   - vehicles: IDM car following (no overlaps), mandatory and discretionary lane changes with indicators, speed by road class,
     slow-down before turns; a mix with real dimensions — sedans, hatchbacks, MPVs, SUVs, pickups (mostly Bay), taxis (generic
     roof sign), vans, box lorries, open-deck lorries, motorcycles, and buses: Singapore 12 m single-deck, 12 m x 4.4 m double-deck,
     18 m articulated; Bay 12 m and 18 m city buses. Generic liveries (white, charcoal skirt, grey-blue band), mostly white /
     silver / grey / black cars. Head, tail, brake, indicator lights, lit bus interiors and destination signs (shader-lit parts),
     at night additive glows (one Points call, also the signal lamps) and headlight pools on the road (one instanced call).
   - buses: Singapore runs the real services (5069 LTA stops, 1206 ordered stop sequences per direction from the busrouter-sg
     data, routed through the road graph between consecutive stops; legs cached and shared), the Bay generates routes along
     named arterials through the real OSM bus stops; buses keep to the kerb lane, pull into the bay (Singapore arterials; a
     38 m lay-by, room for ~2 buses, the rest wait behind), dwell (longer at interchanges and in the morning peak), give way and
     pull out; kerbside stops elsewhere. Shelters (Singapore canopy / US glass shelter) and bays are drawn near the view.
   - density by road group (expressway / arterial / local, each with its own budget) and time of day (peaks 7:30-9:30 and
     17:30-20:00, quiet nights; buses from 5:30 to 0:45 in Singapore, all night in the Bay).
   - performance: full simulation only within ~1.2-3.8 km of the view (the radius follows the camera distance, full density in
     the inner ~400 m, thinning out to the edge), vehicles beyond 0.8 / 1.9 km updated every 2nd / 3rd frame; instanced meshes
     per vehicle type with a far LOD (one box call for all far cars, one for buses / lorries), frustum-culled instance lists,
     ~15-25 draw calls; the network around a far jump is dropped once it grows large.
   The app's cars are switched off while SGR.on: buildCars() is wrapped (it disposes the old InstancedMesh and builds nothing; the
   traffic assignment and the flow overlay still run), so stepCars() has nothing to move; the "车辆 cars" checkbox (#showCars)
   toggles these vehicles instead. Everything lives in one group in the scene, visible only with the "network" toggle
   (showNet && moverGroup.visible); HOOKS.net (network rebuilt / reset) restarts the simulation.
   [nyc/nycapp] New York City (SGR_NYC, tested lazily): yellow cabs by where a car appears (Manhattan below 96th Street ~35 %, upper
   Manhattan ~8 %, by the airports ~12 %, the other boroughs ~3 %, outside the city ~0.5 %; plain taxi yellow, a plain roof light),
   fewer pickups, city speeds (25 mph streets, 30 mph major arterials, 50 mph expressways), the real OSM bus stops of the map
   (TRF_BUS_NY from the New York build's busdata_nyc.js; without it no buses) and city buses in a generic white / blue look.
   Test handle: window.SGTraffic {stats(), warm(seconds), bench(frames), setDensity(k), reset(), enabled, paused, vehicles(),
   signals(), stopNear(x, y), obstacles(x, y, r)}.
   [lm24/drive] v27 crashes: a vehicle hit by the player's car (SGTraffic.hit: an impulse at a point, the impact energy) leaves its
   lane path as a sliding rigid body (mass and yaw inertia by type), stops, sounds its horn (SGAudio.fx 'horn'), shows hazard lights
   and its damage (dents from a per-instance damage code in the shader around the impact point, darker paint), blocks its lane
   (the vehicles behind queue or change lanes; one of them hoots), and is removed after a while; obstacles() returns ids, masses,
   velocities and the crash pose. SGTraffic.suspend = true hides and freezes all traffic (the drive module sets it during an F1
   race). Signal poles and bus shelters are solid (props(x, y, r)); a pole hit hard breaks (breakProp(key): skipped until it is
   repaired after a few minutes).
   [lm27/destruct] v30 thrown traffic: hit(id, jx, jy, px, py, e, jz) takes a vertical impulse; a lifting or very hard hit, or a strong
   toss(x, y, z, r, k) (radial push: the creature's footfalls and strikes, blasts, a falling tower), hands the vehicle to window.SGPhys:
   it tumbles and rolls and settles as a wreck at any pose (drawn here at the body's full rotation; hazards, damage, horn, blocker),
   cleared as the other crashes (or when it sinks). SGTraffic.thrown -> {launched, sunk, flying}. */
